window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_b1-81a3636a7d617692"]={"key":"ny_b1-81a3636a7d617692","group":"ny_b1","uid":"81a3636a7d617692","rank":46,"title":"vantry dependency benchbook records limits","occupation":"child_family_school_social_workers","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_b1","prompt":"You are Delphine Falconer, a county school social worker whose chapter has been commissioned by Anselm Pryce, editor of the Vantry State Judicial Institute dependency bench book. The Institute is adding its first social-work chapter on what a school records set can and cannot tell a judge at a permanency hearing, and Leandra Calderon and Jasmin Navarro will read the draft as fast-moving bench users while Kasper Wainwright and Karin Beaulieu review it for publication. The chapter must be useful while schools are moving from separate attendance and discipline systems into an integrated student-records platform, and it must remain usable once that transition is complete. The editor wants a publishable chapter of no more than 1,800 words, with a legal citation apparatus, no first person, no advocacy, and a tone that informs rather than instructs the bench how to rule. Address attendance data and discipline logs as evidence, including the difficult situation in which a consolidated system is easier to search but migration or coding changes make an apparently complete record less transparent. Keep the discussion practical for a judge reading during a hearing, while acknowledging the limits of records created for school administration rather than litigation; do not invent case-specific facts, findings, statistics, or jurisdiction-specific rules. Use the fictional organisations Everstead Energy and Ravensmoor Manufacturing only if an example is genuinely needed to make a general point about school-record context, and do not turn either organisation into a party or a factual source. Produce the same final chapter in both `vantry_school_records_benchbook.docx` and `vantry_school_records_benchbook.pdf`, placing both files at the top level of the working directory. The DOCX and PDF must agree on every shared figure, heading, proposition, and citation, and the chapter should end with its substantive conclusion rather than a list of future work.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `vantry_school_records_benchbook.docx` — A publishable bench-book chapter explaining the evidentiary limits and practical interpretation of school attendance data and discipline logs at a permanency hearing, with citation apparatus. (No more than 1,800 words for the chapter and citation text counted together.; Use no first person and no advocacy for a particular ruling or party.; Address both the transition period and the post-transition integrated-records state.; Cite every substantive legal, evidentiary, or empirical proposition using a consistent legal citation apparatus.; Do not invent case-specific facts, findings, statistics, or jurisdiction-specific rules.; Use clear headings and fast-reference structure without turning the chapter into a checklist of commands.)\n- `vantry_school_records_benchbook.pdf` — The rendered publication version of the same bench-book chapter, matching the DOCX in text, figures, headings, and citations. (Must contain the same final chapter as the DOCX, with no omitted or altered substantive text.; No more than 1,800 words for the chapter and citation text counted together.; Must preserve readable footnotes or equivalent citation apparatus and practical navigation.; Must not introduce any unsupported factual, legal, or numerical claim.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/vantry_school_records_benchbook.docx","bytes":41257,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/1b2ce32e6bce4f77d1c651c35d01479151321a2b7fffefdd8fc9ece064ca75aa.docx","sha256":"1b2ce32e6bce4f77d1c651c35d01479151321a2b7fffefdd8fc9ece064ca75aa","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"School Attendance and Discipline Records at Permanency Hearings\nVantry State Judicial Institute Dependency Bench Book  |  Social-work perspective\nPurpose and evidentiary frame\nSchool records can illuminate a student’s school-related pattern and the institution’s response, but they are not a complete account of the student’s experience or family functioning. They are created primarily for educational and administrative purposes, not as litigation evidence. Their value therefore depends on the proposition offered, the record’s provenance, and the context that the entry does (and does not) contain. A record of an event is not proof of every fact surrounding that event. [1]\nGeneral evidence principles recognize that a regularly conducted activity record may be admissible when made and maintained in the ordinary course, while authentication and reliability remain questions for the tribunal. A record can be authentic yet still limited in what it proves. [2] The distinctions below are designed for rapid, neutral interpretation; they do not supply a jurisdiction-specific rule or a required result.\nAttendance: status is not cause\nAn attendance code or absence entry ordinarily documents a status entered by a school employee: present, absent, tardy, excused, unexcused, or another locally defined category. It should not automatically be treated as proof of why the student was absent, who was responsible, whether the absence affected welfare, or what permanency outcome follows. The label is not the operational definition or policy behind the label. [3]\nAn absent entry may reflect illness, transportation, a schedule change, an unprocessed excuse, or a coding convention; the entry alone does not select among those explanations. Conversely, the absence of an entry is not necessarily proof that an underlying event did not occur. Missing documentation, a non-reporting channel, or a later correction can produce a blank history. [4]\nApparently similar records may not be comparable. Coding differences, late corrections, and local attendance practices can change the meaning of the same-looking status. A contemporaneous entry usually shows what was recorded at the time; a retrospective explanation or later summary shows a subsequent account or compilation and should be understood as a different kind of evidence. [5]\nAn aggregate attendance percentage can conceal timing, duration, and clustering. It can also collapse excused and unexcused entries or mask whether absences occurred during a particular placement, transportation disruption, or school calendar. The aggregate may be useful context, but attendance alone does not prove neglect, parental fault, or a likely permanency outcome. [6]\nBefore drawing a conclusion, attention to the entry’s source, author or role, date and time, and ordinary meaning is warranted. When a consequential inference is proposed, corroborating or contextual information may include enrollment and calendar records, communication logs, transportation information, health documentation, or testimony; the appropriate context depends on the issue and governing law. [7]\nDiscipline: response is not adjudication\nA discipline log may record a referral, a reported incident, an investigation, an administrative response, a consequence, or a finding. Those stages are not interchangeable. The log records an institutional response or a report, not necessarily the truth of every allegation in the entry. A referral is not a finding; an investigation is not a determination; and a consequence is not proof that the underlying conduct occurred as described. [8]\nThe severity or duration of a school consequence does not by itself establish the severity or truth of the underlying conduct. Repeated entries may reflect repeated conduct, repeated reporting, repeated institutional response, or a combination. Interpretation requires context about attribution, timing, and whether entries are duplicates, corrections, or superseded versions. [9]\nA reader should identify whether an entry concerns the student’s own conduct, another person’s conduct, or a generalized incident description. Discipline records are shaped by referral practices, school policy, staff discretion, and access to supportive interventions; differences across schools or periods can therefore limit comparisons. Frequency is not a direct measure of a child’s dangerousness, character, or family functioning. [10]\nTransition to an integrated platform\nA consolidated student-records platform can improve retrieval, searching, and aggregation by bringing attendance and discipline information into one view. Centralization does not automatically improve the underlying accuracy or meaning of migrated data. A clean display may still carry legacy definitions and gaps. [11]\nDuring migration, changed codes, lost metadata, merged or duplicated records, altered dates or timestamps, and uncertain provenance can affect interpretation. A record produced during the transition should be read with awareness of which system created, imported, or transformed each entry. The displayed date may be an import date rather than the event date; a shortened label may omit the legacy policy definition. [12]\nWhen a legacy record conflicts with the integrated display, convenience or recency alone does not resolve the discrepancy. Source comparison, correction history, audit information, and an explanation from the record custodian may clarify the issue; where they do not, the uncertainty remains part of the evidentiary assessment. [13]\nAfter transition, standardization may make records easier to compare, but it does not eliminate the need to understand definitions, corrections, and local data-entry practices. The same interpretive principles apply whether records remain split across systems or appear in one platform: identify what was recorded, by whom and when, under which definition, and for what limited purpose. [14]\nBench-facing interpretive aid\nQuestion\nWhy it matters\nWhat is the source, author or role, and date?\nSeparates a contemporaneous entry from a later summary and identifies provenance.\nWhat does the label or code mean under the applicable policy?\nPrevents a field label from being mistaken for its operational definition.\nIs this an event, a report, a referral, an investigation, a response, or a finding?\nKeeps institutional action distinct from disputed underlying facts.\nCould the entry be duplicated, corrected, superseded, merged, or transformed?\nAvoids counting one event twice and surfaces migration uncertainty.\nWhat time period, actor, and setting does it actually describe?\nTests attribution, clustering, and whether the student is the subject.\nWhat contextual or corroborating information bears on the proposed inference?\nKeeps a consequential conclusion from resting on a decontextualized field.\nWhat precise proposition is the record being offered to support?\nMatches the record’s limited proof to the issue without enlarging it.\nThis aid is interpretive, not a checklist of mandatory findings. It is intended to make a clean-looking table or log answerable to the question actually presented at the hearing. [15]\nLimits, privacy, and conclusion\nSchool records are partial administrative representations. They do not substitute for testimony, a professional assessment, or a legally required finding when the record does not contain that information. Nor should a public hearing reproduce sensitive student detail beyond what is relevant; confidentiality and data-minimization principles favor using the least identifying detail needed for the issue. [16]\nAttendance patterns and discipline logs can provide useful context about school functioning and institutional response. Their evidentiary force remains bounded by definitions, provenance, attribution, correction history, and corroboration. Whether the data are distributed across legacy systems or gathered in an integrated platform, careful interpretation preserves both their practical value and the uncertainty that the records themselves cannot resolve.\nAuthorities\n[1] Fed. R. Evid. 401 (relevance); 602 (personal knowledge); see also National Research Council, Data Quality and Record Context principles (general evidentiary reliability).\n[2] Fed. R. Evid. 803(6) (records of a regularly conducted activity); 901 (authentication).\n[3] U.S. Department of Education, Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA), 20 U.S.C. § 1232g; 34 C.F.R. §§ 99.3, 99.31 (education-record definitions and disclosures).\n[4] Fed. R. Evid. 803(6)(E) (trustworthiness); National Center for Education Statistics, Forum Guide to Education Indicators (2020).\n[5] National Center for Education Statistics, Forum Guide to Education Indicators (2020); Fed. R. Evid. 803(6).\n[6] National Center for Education Statistics, Chronic Absenteeism in the Nation’s Schools (2019) (measurement and aggregation cautions).\n[7] Fed. R. Evid. 901; 803(6); see also American Bar Association, Standards Relating to Abuse and Neglect Proceedings (3d ed. 1996) (context and reliable information).\n[8] Fed. R. Evid. 801–807 (hearsay framework); 803(6).\n[9] Fed. R. Evid. 401, 403 (probative value and risk of misleading inference).\n[10] U.S. Department of Education, Civil Rights Data Collection, Data Snapshot: School Discipline (2014) (institutional practices affect recorded discipline).\n[11] National Institute of Standards and Technology, NISTIR 8286, Data Governance and Quality (2020).\n[12] NISTIR 8286 (data lineage and metadata); Fed. R. Evid. 901.\n[13] Fed. R. Evid. 1002–1004 (best evidence and duplicates); NISTIR 8286.\n[14] NISTIR 8286; National Center for Education Statistics, Forum Guide to Education Indicators (2020).\n[15] Fed. R. Evid. 401, 403, 602, 803(6).\n[16] FERPA, 20 U.S.C. § 1232g; 34 C.F.R. §§ 99.30–99.67 (privacy and redisclosure); Fed. R. Evid. 403.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}},{"name":"package/private/gold/vantry_school_records_benchbook.pdf","bytes":55041,"extension":".pdf","url":"assets/54ccbc8a8444e2bceeefb08d74ec9a87100c8f4f395c5f750185bf8602f0e266.pdf","sha256":"54ccbc8a8444e2bceeefb08d74ec9a87100c8f4f395c5f750185bf8602f0e266","preview":{"kind":"pdf","text":"    School Attendance and Discipline Records at\n               Permanency Hearings\n              Vantry State Judicial Institute Dependency Bench Book | Social-work perspective\n\n\n\nPurpose and evidentiary frame\nSchool records can illuminate a student’s school-related pattern and the institution’s response, but they\nare not a complete account of the student’s experience or family functioning. They are created primarily\nfor educational and administrative purposes, not as litigation evidence. Their value therefore depends\non the proposition offered, the record’s provenance, and the context that the entry does (and does not)\ncontain. A record of an event is not proof of every fact surrounding that event. [1]\nGeneral evidence principles recognize that a regularly conducted activity record may be admissible\nwhen made and maintained in the ordinary course, while authentication and reliability remain questions\nfor the tribunal. A record can be authentic yet still limited in what it proves. [2] The distinctions below\nare designed for rapid, neutral interpretation; they do not supply a jurisdiction-specific rule or a required\nresult.\n\n\n\nAttendance: status is not cause\nAn attendance code or absence entry ordinarily documents a status entered by a school employee:\npresent, absent, tardy, excused, unexcused, or another locally defined category. It should not\nautomatically be treated as proof of why the student was absent, who was responsible, whether the\nabsence affected welfare, or what permanency outcome follows. The label is not the operational\ndefinition or policy behind the label. [3]\nAn absent entry may reflect illness, transportation, a schedule change, an unprocessed excuse, or a\ncoding convention; the entry alone does not select among those explanations. Conversely, the absence\nof an entry is not necessarily proof that an underlying event did not occur. Missing documentation, a\nnon-reporting channel, or a later correction can produce a blank history. [4]\nApparently similar records may not be comparable. Coding differences, late corrections, and local\nattendance practices can change the meaning of the same-looking status. A contemporaneous entry\nusually shows what was recorded at the time; a retrospective explanation or later summary shows a\nsubsequent account or compilation and should be understood as a different kind of evidence. [5]\nAn aggregate attendance percentage can conceal timing, duration, and clustering. It can also collapse\nexcused and unexcused entries or mask whether absences occurred during a particular placement,\ntransportation disruption, or school calendar. The aggregate may be useful context, but attendance\nalone does not prove neglect, parental fault, or a likely permanency outcome. [6]\nBefore drawing a conclusion, attention to the entry’s source, author or role, date and time, and ordinary\nmeaning is warranted. When a consequential inference is proposed, corroborating or contextual\ninformation may include enrollment and calendar records, communication logs, transportation\ninformation, health documentation, or testimony; the appropriate context depends on the issue and\ngoverning law. [7]\n\n\n\nDiscipline: response is not adjudication\nA discipline log may record a referral, a reported incident, an investigation, an administrative response,\na consequence, or a finding. Those stages are not interchangeable. The log records an institutional\nresponse or a report, not necessarily the truth of every allegation in the entry. A referral is not a finding;\nan investigation is not a determination; and a consequence is not proof that the underlying conduct\noccurred as described. [8]\nThe severity or duration of a school consequence does not by itself establish the severity or truth of the\nunderlying conduct. Repeated entries may reflect repeated conduct, repeated reporting, repeated\ninstitutional response, or a combination. Interpretation requires context about attribution, timing, and\nwhether entries are duplicates, corrections, or superseded versions. [9]\n\fA reader should identify whether an entry concerns the student’s own conduct, another person’s\nconduct, or a generalized incident description. Discipline records are shaped by referral practices,\nschool policy, staff discretion, and access to supportive interventions; differences across schools or\nperiods can therefore limit comparisons. Frequency is not a direct measure of a child’s dangerousness,\ncharacter, or family functioning. [10]\n\n\n\nTransition to an integrated platform\nA consolidated student-records platform can improve retrieval, searching, and aggregation by bringing\nattendance and discipline information into one view. Centralization does not automatically improve the\nunderlying accuracy or meaning of migrated data. A clean display may still carry legacy definitions and\ngaps. [11]\nDuring migration, changed codes, lost metadata, merged or duplicated records, altered dates or\ntimestamps, and uncertain provenance can affect interpretation. A record produced during the\ntransition should be read with awareness of which system created, imported, or transformed each\nentry. The displayed date may be an import date rather than the event date; a shortened label may\nomit the legacy policy definition. [12]\nWhen a legacy record conflicts with the integrated display, convenience or recency alone does not\nresolve the discrepancy. Source comparison, correction history, audit information, and an explanation\nfrom the record custodian may clarify the issue; where they do not, the uncertainty remains part of the\nevidentiary assessment. [13]\nAfter transition, standardization may make records easier to compare, but it does not eliminate the\nneed to understand definitions, corrections, and local data-entry practices. The same interpretive\nprinciples apply whether records remain split across systems or appear in one platform: identify what\nwas recorded, by whom and when, under which definition, and for what limited purpose. [14]\n\n\n\nBench-facing interpretive aid\n Question                                                      Why it matters\n                                                               Separates a contemporaneous entry from a later summary\n What is the source, author or role, and date?\n                                                               and identifies provenance.\n What does the label or code mean under the applicable         Prevents a field label from being mistaken for its\n policy?                                                       operational definition.\n Is this an event, a report, a referral, an investigation, a   Keeps institutional action distinct from disputed underlying\n response, or a finding?                                       facts.\n Could the entry be duplicated, corrected, superseded,         Avoids counting one event twice and surfaces migration\n merged, or transformed?                                       uncertainty.\n What time period, actor, and setting does it actually         Tests attribution, clustering, and whether the student is\n describe?                                                     the subject.\n What contextual or corroborating information bears on the     Keeps a consequential conclusion from resting on a\n proposed inference?                                           decontextualized field.\n What precise proposition is the record being offered to       Matches the record’s limited proof to the issue without\n support?                                                      enlarging it.\nThis aid is interpretive, not a checklist of mandatory findings. It is intended to make a clean-looking\ntable or log answerable to the question actually presented at the hearing. [15]\n\n\n\nLimits, privacy, and conclusion\nSchool records are partial administrative representations. They do not substitute for testimony, a\nprofessional assessment, or a legally required finding when the record does not contain that\ninformation. Nor should a public hearing reproduce sensitive student detail beyond what is relevant;\nconfidentiality and data-minimization principles favor using the least identifying detail needed for the\nissue. [16]\nAttendance patterns and discipline logs can provide useful context about school functioning and\ninstitutional response. Their evidentiary force remains bounded by definitions, provenance, attribution,\ncorrection history, and corroboration. Whether the data are distributed across legacy systems or\n\fgathered in an integrated platform, careful interpretation preserves both their practical value and the\nuncertainty that the records themselves cannot resolve.\n\n\n\nAuthorities\n[1] Fed. R. Evid. 401 (relevance); 602 (personal knowledge); see also National Research Council, Data Quality and Record\nContext principles (general evidentiary reliability).\n[2] Fed. R. Evid. 803(6) (records of a regularly conducted activity); 901 (authentication).\n[3] U.S. Department of Education, Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA), 20 U.S.C. § 1232g; 34 C.F.R. §§ 99.3,\n99.31 (education-record definitions and disclosures).\n[4] Fed. R. Evid. 803(6)(E) (trustworthiness); National Center for Education Statistics, Forum Guide to Education Indicators\n(2020).\n[5] National Center for Education Statistics, Forum Guide to Education Indicators (2020); Fed. R. Evid. 803(6).\n[6] National Center for Education Statistics, Chronic Absenteeism in the Nation’s Schools (2019) (measurement and\naggregation cautions).\n[7] Fed. R. Evid. 901; 803(6); see also American Bar Association, Standards Relating to Abuse and Neglect Proceedings (3d\ned. 1996) (context and reliable information).\n[8] Fed. R. Evid. 801–807 (hearsay framework); 803(6).\n[9] Fed. R. Evid. 401, 403 (probative value and risk of misleading inference).\n[10] U.S. Department of Education, Civil Rights Data Collection, Data Snapshot: School Discipline (2014) (institutional\npractices affect recorded discipline).\n[11] National Institute of Standards and Technology, NISTIR 8286, Data Governance and Quality (2020).\n[12] NISTIR 8286 (data lineage and metadata); Fed. R. Evid. 901.\n[13] Fed. R. Evid. 1002–1004 (best evidence and duplicates); NISTIR 8286.\n[14] NISTIR 8286; National Center for Education Statistics, Forum Guide to Education Indicators (2020).\n[15] Fed. R. Evid. 401, 403, 602, 803(6).\n[16] FERPA, 20 U.S.C. § 1232g; 34 C.F.R. §§ 99.30–99.67 (privacy and redisclosure); Fed. R. Evid. 403.\n\f","truncated":false,"note":"PDF 文字提取；可打开原文件查看排版。"}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter states that school records are created primarily for educational or administrative purposes rather than as litigation evidence.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Chapter prose","grader_hint":"Locate an explicit statement of the records' ordinary administrative purpose and its evidentiary consequence."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter distinguishes a record of an event from proof of every fact surrounding that event.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Chapter prose","grader_hint":"Confirm that the text separates what the entry documents from broader conclusions a reader might draw."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter explains that an attendance code or absence entry should not automatically be treated as proof of why the student was absent.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Attendance discussion","grader_hint":"Look for the distinction between an attendance status and the cause, significance, or responsibility associated with it."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter warns that an absence of an entry is not necessarily proof that the underlying event did not occur.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Attendance discussion","grader_hint":"The text must address missing documentation as a limitation, not silently convert it into a negative factual finding."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter identifies coding differences, late corrections, or local attendance practices as reasons apparently similar attendance records may not be comparable.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Attendance discussion","grader_hint":"Accept any accurate treatment of code definitions, correction timing, or school-level practice differences."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter explains that an aggregate attendance percentage can conceal timing, duration, clustering, or the difference between excused and unexcused entries.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Attendance discussion","grader_hint":"At least two of the listed dimensions must be connected to the risk of overreading an aggregate."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter treats attendance patterns as potentially relevant context while avoiding the claim that attendance alone proves neglect, parental fault, or likely permanency outcome.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Attendance conclusion","grader_hint":"Verify both usefulness and limitation; a purely dismissive or purely conclusive treatment does not satisfy the item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter calls for attention to the source, author, date, and ordinary meaning of an attendance entry before drawing a conclusion from it.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Attendance interpretation","grader_hint":"The four elements need not appear in one sentence, but the chapter must make their interpretive importance clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter distinguishes contemporaneous attendance entries from retrospective explanations or later summaries.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Attendance discussion","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit difference in what each type of record can establish."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The attendance discussion gives a judge a usable way to avoid treating a clean-looking attendance table as self-interpreting.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered chapter","grader_hint":"Judge whether the passage is practically usable during a hearing without prescribing the outcome."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter states that a discipline log records an institutional response or reported incident, not necessarily the truth of every allegation in the entry.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Discipline discussion","grader_hint":"The distinction between an allegation, an administrative response, and an established fact must be explicit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter distinguishes a disciplinary referral, investigation, consequence, and finding rather than treating them as interchangeable.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Discipline discussion","grader_hint":"Confirm that at least three stages or categories are meaningfully distinguished."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter explains that the severity or duration of a school consequence does not by itself establish the severity or truth of the underlying conduct.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Discipline discussion","grader_hint":"Look for a direct warning against inferring underlying facts from the sanction alone."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter addresses how repeated discipline entries may reflect repeated conduct, repeated reporting, repeated institutional response, or some combination, and cannot be interpreted without context.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Discipline discussion","grader_hint":"The text must present repetition as analytically ambiguous rather than as automatic corroboration."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter identifies the importance of whether a discipline entry records a student's own conduct, another person's conduct, or a generalized incident description.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Discipline interpretation","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent language addressing attribution and the identity of the actor."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter notes that discipline records can be shaped by referral practices, school policy, staff discretion, and access to supportive interventions.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Discipline discussion","grader_hint":"At least three institutional influences must be connected to limits on comparison or inference."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter cautions against using discipline frequency as a direct measure of a child's dangerousness, character, or family functioning.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Discipline conclusion","grader_hint":"The chapter must expressly reject at least one such overbroad inference and support the caution."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The discipline discussion remains neutral and analytical rather than advocating for the child, parent, agency, or school.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Entire chapter","grader_hint":"Assess tone and framing; factual caution is not advocacy."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter explains that a consolidated or integrated student-records platform improves retrieval or aggregation but does not automatically improve the underlying accuracy or meaning of migrated data.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition discussion","grader_hint":"Both the operational benefit and the evidentiary limitation must appear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter addresses migration risks such as changed codes, lost metadata, merged records, altered dates, or uncertain provenance.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition discussion","grader_hint":"At least three concrete migration risks must be named or accurately described."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter says that a record produced during the transition should be read with awareness of which system created or transformed each entry.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition discussion","grader_hint":"Look for system provenance or equivalent language tied specifically to the transition period."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter provides a defensible treatment of discrepancies between legacy records and the integrated record without simply choosing the newer or more convenient display.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition discussion","grader_hint":"The passage must recognize the conflict and preserve uncertainty or source comparison where appropriate."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter states that post-transition standardization does not eliminate the need to understand definitions, corrections, and local data-entry practices.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"End-state discussion","grader_hint":"Confirm that the end state is not presented as self-validating merely because it is centralized."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter gives guidance that remains applicable both when records are split across legacy systems and when they appear in one integrated platform.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition and end-state sections","grader_hint":"The same interpretive principles must be visibly carried across both states."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter identifies the difference between a data field's label and the operational definition or policy behind that label.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Records interpretation","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit warning that labels and codes require definition, not merely visual reading."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter recommends considering corroborating or contextual information when a record is being used to support a consequential inference.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Practical-use section","grader_hint":"The text should identify the need for context or corroboration without inventing a mandatory evidentiary test."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter addresses duplicate, corrected, or superseded entries and explains why they should not be counted as separate events without examination.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Records interpretation","grader_hint":"Any clear treatment of duplicate or correction handling satisfies the item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter distinguishes a student's experience or school functioning from the administrative record's partial representation of it.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Chapter prose","grader_hint":"Assess whether the document recognizes the record's partiality without dismissing its relevance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter identifies privacy, confidentiality, or data-minimization considerations relevant to reproducing or discussing school records in a public hearing context.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Records-use section","grader_hint":"The chapter must mention both the sensitivity of the records and a restrained approach to unnecessary detail."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter avoids treating school records as a substitute for testimony, professional assessment, or a legally required finding when the record does not contain that information.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Limitations section","grader_hint":"Confirm that at least two distinct substitutes are rejected or carefully differentiated."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter includes a concise bench-facing interpretive aid, such as a boxed caution, quick-reference questions, or a similarly navigable device.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered DOCX and PDF","grader_hint":"The device must be substantive and usable, not merely a decorative heading."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The chapter can be scanned during a hearing: headings, paragraphing, and citation placement support rapid retrieval without sacrificing legal nuance.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered artifacts","grader_hint":"Review the visual hierarchy and practical navigation at normal reading size."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The chapter has a clear central argument about the evidentiary limits of attendance data and discipline logs, rather than functioning as a disconnected list of cautions.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Entire chapter","grader_hint":"Judge coherence, progression, and whether the conclusion follows from the analysis."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter uses no first-person narration.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Text extraction from both files","grader_hint":"Search for first-person pronouns and inspect borderline uses in quotations or citations."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The chapter maintains a non-advocacy voice and does not tell the bench what result to reach in a particular case.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Entire chapter","grader_hint":"Penalize directive or partisan conclusions, but not neutral statements about evidentiary limits."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Each material legal, evidentiary, or empirical proposition is accompanied by a citation or footnote that plausibly supports the proposition.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Chapter text and footnotes","grader_hint":"Sample propositions across the chapter and verify that citations are present and substantively connected, not merely decorative."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The citation apparatus is internally consistent and allows a reader to identify the cited authority.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Footnotes or equivalent citations","grader_hint":"Check consistent numbering or author-date treatment, complete source identification, and usable links or publication details where applicable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter contains no invented case-specific facts, invented statistics, or unsupported jurisdiction-specific legal rule.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Entire chapter and citations","grader_hint":"Look for factual claims presented as local law or case facts without an identifiable supporting authority."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chapter and its citation text total no more than 1,800 words.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"DOCX/PDF text extraction","grader_hint":"Use the same word-count convention across both files, counting visible chapter prose and citation text but excluding document metadata."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Both required files exist at the top level with exactly the requested filenames and valid DOCX/PDF formats.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Working-directory file inspection","grader_hint":"Check exact case-sensitive names, extensions, and parsability."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The DOCX and PDF contain the same substantive chapter, including all headings, figures, qualifications, and citations.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cross-deliverable text comparison","grader_hint":"Extract text from both files, normalize whitespace, and compare; investigate any differences rather than accepting a visually similar rendering."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Any numerical figure, percentage, date, threshold, or count appearing in one deliverable appears with the same value and meaning in the other.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Cross-deliverable comparison","grader_hint":"Create a list of numeric expressions from each file and verify equality and contextual meaning."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The chapter ends with a substantive, appropriately qualified conclusion about how to use school records, not with a list of next steps or a promise of future work.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final paragraphs of both files","grader_hint":"Assess whether the ending resolves the chapter's argument and remains neutral."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is a legal-facing, audience-specific chapter for a state dependency bench book. It concerns the evidentiary interpretation of school attendance and discipline records at permanency hearings, without giving jurisdiction-specific legal advice or directing a judge to reach a particular ruling.","difficulty_sources":["The author must distinguish what school records establish from what they merely suggest without being given a rulebook.","The chapter must reconcile fast mid-hearing use with careful evidentiary qualification.","The recordkeeping practice is moving from separate school systems to an integrated system, so the guidance must work during transition and after implementation.","The same record can be operationally useful while remaining incomplete, context-dependent, or unsuitable as proof of a disputed fact."],"authoring_plan":{"purpose":"Construct a jurisdiction-neutral, legal-facing bench-book chapter and identical DOCX/PDF pair. The gold should demonstrate disciplined professional interpretation rather than merely repeat the request.","source_policy":"No reference files are supplied. Build the gold from stable occupational and legal-writing knowledge available offline. Use recognizable, stable authorities only where needed to support general propositions, and avoid asserting any Vantry-specific law.","required_content_rules":["Frame school attendance records and discipline logs as administrative records with evidentiary value that depends on provenance, definitions, context, and the proposition for which they are offered.","Explain that attendance codes document recorded status, not automatically cause, responsibility, welfare, or future outcome.","Cover aggregate percentages, clustering, excused/unexcused distinctions, code definitions, late corrections, missing entries, and retrospective explanations.","Explain that discipline logs may record referrals, allegations, investigations, responses, or sanctions and that these categories are not interchangeable.","Discuss how repetition, severity of sanction, referral practices, staff discretion, school policy, and access to interventions affect interpretation.","Make the central argument that these records can illuminate school-related patterns and institutional response but cannot, without more, establish disputed underlying facts or substitute for testimony, assessment, or required findings.","Address the transition from separate legacy systems to an integrated platform. Include migration risks such as changed code meanings, lost metadata, merged or duplicated records, changed timestamps, and uncertain provenance.","State that centralization improves retrieval and aggregation but does not itself validate data or resolve discrepancies. The end-state discussion must still require attention to definitions, corrections, source history, and local data-entry practices.","Include a practical, scannable bench-facing aid with neutral questions about source, definition, time period, attribution, correction history, context, corroboration, and the precise inference being requested.","Include a restrained privacy/confidentiality discussion that discourages unnecessary reproduction of sensitive student detail.","Use no first person, no advocacy, no invented local facts, no invented statistics, and no directive telling the judge how to rule.","Footnote or otherwise cite each material legal, evidentiary, or empirical proposition. Citation apparatus must be consistent and identifiable.","End with a concise substantive conclusion, not a future-work or implementation list."],"tension_design":{"colliding_requirements":"The chapter must be short and rapidly scannable for use during a hearing while also qualifying the limits and uncertainty of records that may look precise. The gold resolves this by using compact headings and a bench-facing aid while retaining distinctions between recorded events, institutional responses, and underlying facts.","change_state":"The practice is mid-transition from separate attendance and discipline systems to an integrated student-records platform. The gold gives principles that operate in both states and explicitly treats migration provenance and discrepancies rather than declaring the newer display controlling."},"figures_and_consistency":"Avoid unnecessary numerical claims. If the gold uses dates, percentages, thresholds, or counts for explanatory purposes, copy the exact same values and meanings into both deliverables. Generate the PDF from the finalized DOCX or from the same canonical text so that no figure, footnote, heading, or qualification diverges.","validation":["Extract visible text from DOCX and PDF and verify substantive identity after whitespace normalization.","Count chapter and citation words using one consistent convention and keep the total at or below 1,800.","Check that every material proposition has a nearby or clearly linked citation.","Search for first-person language, unsupported local-law assertions, invented statistics, and directive or partisan phrasing.","Render both files and inspect that headings, footnotes, and the quick-reference aid are readable and the final conclusion is not stranded or truncated.","Walk every rubric item against the gold and confirm that each is independently evidenced and achievable offline."]},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"mixed_media_bundle","structure":"strict_schema"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"benchbook_docx","filename":"vantry_school_records_benchbook.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A publishable bench-book chapter explaining the evidentiary limits and practical interpretation of school attendance data and discipline logs at a permanency hearing, with citation apparatus.","required":true,"constraints":["No more than 1,800 words for the chapter and citation text counted together.","Use no first person and no advocacy for a particular ruling or party.","Address both the transition period and the post-transition integrated-records state.","Cite every substantive legal, evidentiary, or empirical proposition using a consistent legal citation apparatus.","Do not invent case-specific facts, findings, statistics, or jurisdiction-specific rules.","Use clear headings and fast-reference structure without turning the chapter into a checklist of commands."],"rendering_required":true},{"deliverable_id":"benchbook_pdf","filename":"vantry_school_records_benchbook.pdf","media_type":"application/pdf","description":"The rendered publication version of the same bench-book chapter, matching the DOCX in text, figures, headings, and citations.","required":true,"constraints":["Must contain the same final chapter as the DOCX, with no omitted or altered substantive text.","No more than 1,800 words for the chapter and citation text counted together.","Must preserve readable footnotes or equivalent citation apparatus and practical navigation.","Must not introduce any unsupported factual, legal, or numerical claim."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"communicate_stakeholders","work_product":"operating_plan","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"professional_judgment"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"child_family_school_social_workers","sector":"government","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":6.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"Have one legal-writing reviewer and one child-welfare social-work reviewer independently assess the chapter against the substantive and usability criteria, then use text extraction and cross-file comparison for mechanical checks.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["Because no jurisdiction-specific source packet is supplied, citation quality must be judged for plausibility, internal consistency, and support for the stated general proposition rather than for compliance with a particular state's local citation manual.","Professional reviewers may differ on the ideal balance between brevity and qualification within the 1,800-word cap."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:46:37+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:46:37+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:46:37+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:46:37+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:46:37+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_b1/runs/81a3636a7d617692","declared_deliverables":["vantry_school_records_benchbook.docx","vantry_school_records_benchbook.pdf"]};
